schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Building Trust in Community Finance"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A guide to building and protecting the trust a local node's community
    finance (timebank, local currency, shared treasury) runs on, for
    organizers already working in regen/web3. Distinguishes verification
    (proof of what happened) from trust (belief people will do what they
    say), names three kinds of trust to build deliberately (competence,
    integrity, benevolence), lists concrete trust-building practices
    (transparency by default, consistency, owning mistakes, keeping
    commitments, inclusion, celebrating trustworthy behavior), addresses the
    "trust velocity" bootstrapping paradox, names early warning signs of
    trust breakdown, and gives a five-step repair process after a breach.
    Cites Fukuyama's social-trust thesis and Grassroots Economics' Sarafu
    Network as worked evidence.
  audience: organizers running community treasuries, timebanks, or local currencies
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      technology gives verification, not trust — the article treats a smart
      contract as able to enforce a rule but not make people want to show
      up, arguing nodes must build social trust rather than substitute
      technology for trust not yet earned.
    - >-
      trust velocity paradox — you need trust to run community finance, but
      you need to run community finance together to build trust; resolved by
      starting low-stakes and graduating trust over time.
  related_concepts:
    - three-kinds-of-trust-competence-integrity-benevolence
  related_resources:
    - "Grassroots Economics: Sarafu Network"
    - Safe (multisig wallets)
    - Human Passport
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/building-trust.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-06b003d4c0b2
  source_lineage: building-trust.md
  notes: |
    # assessed-but-excluded
    - No individual community members named; no exact locations, TEK, or
      MRV/carbon claims. The article does give specific treasury/governance
      configuration advice (e.g. 3-of-5 multisig signer setups), which is a
      funding/governance-recommendation trigger — see the companion
      public-use-boundary object.
    - Fukuyama's book and the Sarafu Network survey are modeled as their own
      claim-evidence objects rather than folded into this summary, since each
      carries independent, checkable evidentiary weight.
